# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Line-ending policy for SerenSetupScripts
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#  WHY THIS FILE IS MORE THAN `* text=auto`:
#
#  `text=auto` normalizes to LF *in the repository* but checks out with
#  NATIVE endings. On a Windows checkout that means every .sh comes back as
#  CRLF, gets edited, and gets committed with CRLF still in it.
#
#  A .sh file with CRLF does not merely look untidy - it DOES NOT PARSE.
#  bash sees the trailing \r as part of the token:
#
#      seren-margin-setup.sh: line 43: syntax error near unexpected token `$'{\r''
#
#  This has now bitten twice. The second time, all seven seren-*-setup.sh
#  installers plus seren-install-lib.sh plus setup-seren-python-service.sh
#  were simultaneously unrunnable on every Linux and macOS box - which is
#  every Jetson in the cluster - while looking completely fine in an editor
#  and in a GitHub diff.
#
#  The explicit `eol=` rules below beat the native-checkout default, so a
#  Windows working tree gets LF for shell scripts no matter what. This is
#  the recurrence fix; the file conversions were the one-time repair.
#
#  Guard for the next time anyway - it costs nothing and it's the only
#  check that catches this before a Jetson does:
#      bash -n path/to/script.sh
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Sensible default for everything not named below.
* text=auto

# ── Shell: LF, always, everywhere ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Non-negotiable. CRLF here is a parse error, not a style preference.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# ── PowerShell: deliberately NOT pinned ───────────────────────────────────
# There is no *.ps1 rule here, and that's on purpose.
#
# PowerShell reads LF perfectly well - CRLF is a preference for .ps1, not a
# requirement, which is the whole difference from .sh above. The tree is
# currently mixed (some CRLF, some LF) and it works fine either way.
#
# Pinning eol=crlf would rewrite every LF file on the next checkout: a
# whole-file churn across nine scripts, blowing up `git blame`, to fix
# nothing. `* text=auto` handles them well enough. Only pin an ending when
# the wrong one BREAKS something.

# ── Config / docs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# YAML and systemd units are read by Linux tooling; keep them LF too.
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.plist   text eol=lf
*.md      text

# ── Binary: never touched ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.whl   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
